#
# Clean conformance fixture dogfooding the NON-EXACT polarity of the probability-model
# lowering seam: a reasoning-facing math:FactorGraph whose math:probabilityModelLowering
# into logic:DependencyModel carries a NON-exact logic:preservationKind
# (logic:SoundUnderApproximation) WITH its dropped constructs enumerated in the loss ledger
# through logic:unsupportedConstruct (the string-valued loss-ledger property). The factor
# product is unnormalized, so its intractable partition function (normalizer Z) and its
# continuous factors cannot be carried through the discrete weighted-model-counting target
# — those drops are DISCLOSED, never silent (Principle 17). The lowering gates
# (math:MissingProbabilityModelLowering, math:ExactPreservationViolated) are native join
# checks and carry no SHACL target shape; math:FactorGraph (a math:MathematicalObject) has
# no SHACL target shape either. It MUST raise NO probability failure class.
# Bound by tests/example-conformance.ttl (ex:ecDependencyModelApproximateLossy).
@prefix math:  <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/math/> .
@prefix logic: <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/logic/> .
@prefix ex:    <http://example.org/math/> .

ex:unnormalizedFactorGraph a math:FactorGraph ;
    math:dependencyGraph ex:factorGraphStructure ;
    # A DECLARED lowering into the logic: dependency-model target, whose preservation
    # polarity is honestly SOUND-UNDER-APPROXIMATION (not exact): the discrete weighted
    # factor product over-/under-approximates the unnormalized continuous model.
    math:probabilityModelLowering logic:DependencyModel ;
    logic:preservationKind logic:SoundUnderApproximation ;
    # The constructs the lowering cannot carry, enumerated as recorded loss (multi-valued,
    # string-typed loss-ledger property), never dropped silently.
    logic:unsupportedConstruct
        "partition-function normalizer Z (intractable, so the product is left unnormalized)" ,
        "continuous factors (approximated by discrete weighted-model-counting factors)" .

ex:factorGraphStructure a math:MathematicalObject .
